 The problem is a classic example of a binomial probability problem. The coin flips are independent, and the probability of getting heads on any single flip is \( \frac{1}{2} \). We need to find the probability of getting at least 6 heads in 10 flips. This is equivalent to the sum of the probabilities of getting exactly 6, 7, 8, 9, and 10 heads. The probability of getting exactly \( k \) heads in 10 flips is given by the binomial probability formula: \[
P(X = k) = \binom{10}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10}
\] where \(\binom{10}{k}\) is the binomial coefficient. The probability of getting at least 6 heads is then: \[
P(X \geq 6) = \sum_{k=6}^{10} \binom{10}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10}
\] </think> <answer> \frac{193}{512} </answer>

